<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Samudera Pasai Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1267 CE – 1521 CE (approx.) · capital Pasai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first securely dated Muslim kingdom of Southeast Asia, a pepper port in northern Sumatra visited by Marco Polo and Ibn Battuta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Samudera Pasai arose on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhokseumawe">Lhokseumawe</a>, in the late 13th century. Its first Muslim ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Malik al-<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salihids">Salih</a>, is known from his tombstone dated 1297, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-raja-pasai">Hikayat Raja Pasai</a> tells of his conversion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> stopped at Samara in 1292. It later absorbed neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peureulak-sultanate">Peureulak</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pasai exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a> and issued gold coins, among the earliest in the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1345 and praised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Malik al-Zahir as a learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> ruler who debated with scholars. Pasai became a centre from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Malay letters spread across the archipelago.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Pasai was raided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> around 1350 and later overshadowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>. The Portuguese seized it in 1521, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate</a> conquered it in 1524. Its royal tombstones, some of them carved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, remain important early evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/samudera-pasai-sultanate.jpg" alt="Samudera Pasai Sultanate" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaes Visscher II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaes_Visscher_Indiae_Orientalis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1267 CE – 1521 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pasai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Malik al-Salih, al-Malik al-Zahir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Shafi'i)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pasai, Samudra Pasai, Samudera</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1394&amp;focus=samudera-pasai-sultanate" aria-label="Samudera Pasai Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7375 1986 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Samudera Pasai Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7695 2242L7713 2255M7695 2242L8112 2721M7695 2242L7643 1242M7695 2242L4773 1044M7695 2242L7642 2226"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7713 2255h0M8112 2721h0M7643 1242h0M4773 1044h0M7642 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7695 2242h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7695 2242h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1394&amp;focus=samudera-pasai-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
